      "writtenOn": "2021-07-22T19:39:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You are right to be concerned. In google3 they specifically make sure their hash maps aren\u0027t consistent from run to run, to avoid writing tests that accidentally rely on not-guaranteed hash table behavior.\n\nIt might be accidentally stable if your hashing function is particularly weak. I thought it might accidentally be relying on the low bits of the pointer as the hash key, but I looked into it and you should be getting something decent:\n\nhttps://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/blob/ee7242c662cd4d93e5af622c69d0db7f57d35505/libcxx/include/__functional/hash.h#L437\n\nMaybe skslc is just that predictable in its allocations?\nAt any rate, yes, unfortunately, we need to do *something* to make sure the error is stable. We could do something corny like pick the function in the cycle with the lexicographically-lowest description() and always start the cycle listing from there.",
